// Package docs turns the indexed graph into a document a person can read, and
// does it deterministically enough to commit the result.
//
// # Why this exists
//
// Two kinds of architecture documentation are common and both fail. Hand-written
// documents describe a system that stopped existing three refactors ago. Prose
// generated by a language model reads beautifully and cites nothing, so a reader
// who doubts a sentence has no way to check it and no way to tell which
// sentences changed because the code changed.
//
// This package takes the third option: every node and every edge in every
// diagram is DERIVED from a fact in the index, and carries the file and line
// that fact came from. A reader who does not believe a arrow can open the file.
//
// # Determinism is the feature
//
// The same index produces byte-identical output, so the document can live in the
// repository and its diff is a real diff — the lines that changed are the
// architecture that changed. That is a property an LLM cannot offer at any
// temperature, and it is the reason to prefer a derived document to a written
// one even when the written one reads better.
//
// Concretely: nothing here iterates a map to decide an order, and nothing here
// writes a timestamp into the document body. Inputs are re-sorted on entry
// rather than trusted to arrive sorted, so a caller supplying rows in a
// different order still gets the same bytes.
//
// # Components are DECLARED, never inferred
//
// Where .cortex.yml declares a component model, that model is used. Where it
// does not, this package SAYS SO and stops, rather than clustering packages and
// presenting the result as the architecture. The argument is already made in
// internal/capability/architecture's package doc and it applies with more force
// here: a component model RepoCortex guessed would be a description of the code's
// current shape, and a diagram of a description of the code is a picture of the
// code with fewer details. Naming it "the architecture" would be the same
// unverifiable assertion the competing tools ship, dressed as a diagram.
//
// The endpoint flows and the inheritance view are different: those are read
// directly off EXPOSES, CALLS, READS, WRITES, IMPLEMENTS and EXTENDS edges, each
// of which is a fact somebody's parser produced with evidence attached. They are
// rendered whether or not a component model exists.
//
// # Bounded
//
// A 10,000-symbol repository must not produce a 10,000-node diagram; nobody
// reads that and no renderer lays it out. Every section has a cap, the ordering
// that decides what survives the cap is deterministic and documented, and what
// was dropped is stated in the document rather than silently missing. A
// truncated diagram that admits it is truncated is usable; one that does not is
// a lie with a legend.
//
// # What this deliberately does not do
//
// It does not write files. Generate returns strings; where they land is the
// caller's decision and the caller's error to wrap.
//
// It does not render Mermaid to images, and it does not depend on a Mermaid
// version's newer syntax. The emitted diagrams use `graph`, `flowchart` and
// `classDiagram` with quoted labels and nothing else, because a diagram that
// only renders on the latest renderer is a diagram that does not render in the
// reader's code host.
//
// It does not use internal/graph's traversal. That traversal is
// confidence-weighted and budget-bounded by design, which is right for context
// assembly and wrong here: the endpoint flow needs one specific shape
// (EXPOSES, then CALLS to a bounded depth, then READS/WRITES) and needs the
// result to be a pure function of the edge set. Re-deriving that locally from
// edges already read is both simpler and the only version that can promise
// byte-identical output. See ports.go.
package docs
